<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/intermediate/atoms-to-crystals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    "Structure determines properties" — and structure starts at the atom: <strong>bonding type
    sets the ceiling, crystal packing sets the mechanics, and defects — counterintuitively —
    deliver the behaviour engineers actually use</strong>. Perfect crystals are a laboratory
    curiosity; useful materials are organised imperfection. If the foundational tier gave you
    the property tables (stiffness, strength, conductivity, toughness), this page rebuilds
    them from first principles: by the end you should be able to look at a material, ask what
    its atoms are doing at three scales — bond, lattice, defect — and predict most of its
    mechanical personality.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="bonds, lattices, the defect gift, order levels" widget="equationStep" config={atomsCfg} />

  <h2>Four bonds, four property sets</h2>
  <p>
    Every solid is held together by some mix of four interactions, and each buys a distinct
    property bundle. <strong>Metallic bonding</strong>: atoms shed their outer electrons into
    a shared "sea" that is free to move — hence electrical and thermal conductivity — while
    the non-directional sea lets planes of atoms slide past one another without destroying
    the bond, hence ductility. <strong>Ionic bonding</strong>: electrons transfer (sodium
    gives chlorine one), producing hard, often high-melting crystals that conduct only when
    molten or dissolved — and that shatter rather than dent, for a subtle reason below.
    <strong>Covalent bonding</strong>: electrons shared along specific directions, giving the
    stiffest materials known — diamond's carbon network resists any local rearrangement, so
    it is the hardest natural substance and a terrible electrical conductor (electrons are
    all busy). <strong>Secondary (van der Waals) bonding</strong>: weak flickering
    electrostatic attractions between neutral atoms and molecules — individually feeble, but
    they are the glue between polymer chains and between the layers of graphite; a gecko's
    foot exploits exactly these forces, summed over billions of spatula hairs.
  </p>
  <p>
    The clearest demonstration that bonding geometry, not bond strength, governs behaviour:
    carbon. Diamond and graphite are both pure carbon with strong covalent bonds, yet one is
    the hardest substance known and the other writes on paper. Diamond is a 3-D network
    (each carbon bonded tetrahedrally to four others — no weak direction); graphite is flat
    sheets of carbon bonded strongly within the sheet but only van der Waals-weak between
    sheets — so sheets shear off (pencil lead) and slide as a lubricant, while within each
    sheet graphite is actually stiffer than diamond. Same element; the architecture of the
    bonding does everything.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> ionic crystals are brittle because ionic bonds are weak.
  They are among the strongest bonds in the table (rock salt melts at 801 °C). The
  brittleness is geometric: shear the planes of NaCl by one ion spacing and you bring
  positive face against positive face — the repulsion cracks the crystal open. Copper can
  deform because its electron sea forgives any slide; rock salt cannot because its charges
  do not.</p>

  <h2>Lattices and unit cells: the 14 ways to fill space</h2>
  <p>
    A crystal is an arrangement of atoms that repeats by translation. The smallest repeating
    block is the <strong>unit cell</strong>, and in 1848 Auguste Bravais proved that
    three-dimensional periodicity admits exactly <strong>14 distinct lattices</strong> — every
    crystal in nature, from table salt to protein, must obey one of them. Metals mostly pick
    three. <strong>FCC</strong> (face-centred cubic — copper, aluminium, nickel, gold, silver):
    atoms at cube corners and face centres, 4 atoms per cell, coordination number 12, packing
    fraction 74%. <strong>HCP</strong> (hexagonal close-packed — zinc, magnesium, titanium):
    the same 74% efficiency, arranged in hexagonal layers. <strong>BCC</strong>
    (body-centred cubic — iron at room temperature, tungsten, molybdenum): a slightly looser
    68%, coordination 8.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worked example — the packing fraction of FCC. Atoms touch along the face diagonal: a
    diagonal spans one radius at each corner plus the full face-centre atom, so
    4r = a√2, giving a = 2√2·r. Cell volume = a³ = 16√2·r³. The cell contains 4 whole atoms
    (8 corners × ⅛ + 6 faces × ½), volume 4 × (4πr³/3) = 16πr³/3. Packing fraction =
    (16πr³/3) ⁄ (16√2·r³) = π ⁄ (3√2) ≈ 0.74. That number — 74% — is a mathematical limit:
    Kepler conjectured, and only in 2017 was the proof finally certified, that no arrangement
    of equal spheres can beat it. Grocers stacking oranges had known it for centuries.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the lattice choice foretells the mechanics. FCC metals have many densely packed
    planes and directions in which planes can slip — 12 independent slip systems — which is
    why copper, aluminium and gold are soft and famously ductile. HCP metals have few
    effective slip systems, so zinc and magnesium are brittle along certain directions.
    BCC iron is strong but turns brittle in cold water — the famous Liberty ships of WWII
    fracturing in the North Atlantic were BCC steel below its ductile-to-brittle transition
    temperature. One line of crystallography, read in advance, is a safety briefing.
  </p>

  <h2>Planes and Miller indices: addressing the crystal</h2>
  <p>
    Because properties differ by direction, crystallographers need a notation for planes:
    <strong>Miller indices</strong>. Take the plane's intercepts with the cell axes, invert
    them, clear fractions, and bracket the result: the cube faces are (100), the face
    diagonal plane (110), the close-packed plane in FCC (111). Directions get the same
    arithmetic in square brackets. The notation looks pedantic until you need it: catalysis,
    etching rates, X-ray analysis and slip all run on specific planes. In the FCC metals
    above, slip happens preferentially on {111} planes along ⟨110⟩ directions — the densest
    riding surfaces in the lattice — and whole technologies (semiconductor wafers are cut
    and cleaved along (100) and (111), which is why the notches on a chip's edge matter)
    depend on knowing it.
  </p>

  <h2>X-ray diffraction: how we know any of this</h2>
  <p>
    Atoms are too small for microscopes of visible light — but X-rays have wavelengths of
    about the atomic spacing, so a crystal acts as a three-dimensional diffraction grating
    for them. Bragg's law (1912–13, father, son and the crystal lattice) gives the condition
    for a diffracted beam: <strong>nλ = 2d·sinθ</strong> — waves reflected from successive
    atomic planes reinforce only when the extra path 2d·sinθ is a whole number of
    wavelengths.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worked example: copper's Kα X-ray line has λ = 0.154 nm. Aim it at a crystal whose
    planes lie d = 0.308 nm apart. First-order reflection (n = 1): sinθ = λ ⁄ 2d =
    0.154 ⁄ 0.616 = 0.25, so θ ≈ 14.5° (the reflected beam leaves at 29° from the incident
    beam — mind the factor of 2). Rotate the crystal and record the angles where intensity
    spikes: each spike is a family of planes, and the whole set of angles is a fingerprint.
    This is how the structure of DNA, penicillin, proteins and every new battery material
    is confirmed — X-ray diffraction is the single most consequential measurement technique
    in materials science, and it works because crystals are periodic: a disordered material
    blurs the pattern into broad halos, which is itself diagnostic.
  </p>

  <h2>Defects: the imperfections everything depends on</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the founding paradox of the field. A <em>perfect</em> crystal should resist
    shear with theoretical strength on the order of G/2π — for copper, something like
    several GPa. Real copper yields at about 30 MPa: roughly a hundredfold weaker. The gap
    is not contamination or sloppiness; it is one specific defect. A <strong>dislocation</strong>
    is a line along which the crystal is offset — imagine a rug with a wrinkle that you can
    push across the floor, moving one carpet-row at a time, instead of dragging the whole
    rug. That is how metal planes slip: cheaply, one atomic row at a time. Dislocations are
    why metals are ductile at all, and their multiplication and entanglement under
    deformation is why a bent paperclip stiffens at the crease (work-hardening: you made a
    traffic jam of dislocations).
  </p>
  <p>
    Once you see strength as dislocation control, the whole metallurgy toolkit becomes
    legible. <strong>Alloying</strong> pins dislocations with differently-sized solute atoms
    and their strain fields (bronze beats copper; that is the tin doing). <strong>Grain
    boundaries</strong> — the borders between neighbouring crystals — are obstacles too, and
    the <strong>Hall–Petch relation</strong> makes it quantitative: yield strength rises
    roughly as one over the square root of grain size. Same steel, finer grains, stronger
    part. <strong>Point defects</strong> run the chemistry: vacancies (missing atoms, present
    at equilibrium — near the melting point, perhaps one atom site in ten thousand is
    empty) are how atoms diffuse, and diffusion is what carburises steel gears, sinters
    ceramic powders and dopes silicon. Speaking of which: a semiconductor is a crystal
    whose entire value proposition is deliberate point defects — one phosphorus atom per
    million silicon atoms donates a mobile electron and changes conductivity by orders of
    magnitude. The information age is defect engineering with sub-parts-per-million
    precision.
  </p>

  <h2>Three states of order: single crystal, polycrystal, amorphous</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Single crystals</strong> (one lattice, no internal boundaries): semiconductor
    wafers must be them (a grain boundary across a transistor is a short circuit), and jet
    turbine blades are grown as single crystals by directional solidification because creep
    — the slow void-growth that kills blades at 1,100 °C — happens at grain boundaries.
    Eliminate the boundaries, eliminate the failure site. <strong>Polycrystals</strong>
    (millions of tiny grains): virtually all engineering metal — cast, rolled, welded — with
    grain size as a design dial (Hall–Petch). <strong>Amorphous solids</strong> (no repeating
    order — a frozen liquid): oxide glasses, most plastics, and metallic glasses made by
    cooling fast enough to outrun crystallisation. Amorphous metals are extraordinary — no
    dislocations to slip, so they are elastic to a striking degree — but they shatter
    suddenly because there are no grain boundaries to arrest a crack.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> glass is transparent <em>because</em> it is amorphous.
  Transparency is mostly electronic: glass has no energy states that absorb visible
  photons, and crystalline quartz and diamond are just as transparent. What amorphousness
  contributes is the absence of grain boundaries and pores to scatter light — polycrystalline
  ceramics look milky precisely because their boundaries scatter. Order controls opacity's
  scattering term, not the absorption term.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Anisotropy and texture.</strong> A single crystal has different stiffness in
    different directions (elastic anisotropy of copper is a factor of ~3), but a random
    polycrystal averages it out. Processing, however, is not random — rolling and drawing
    align grains into <strong>texture</strong>, which is why rolled sheet is stiffer along
    the roll than across it and why the deep-drawn walls of a drink can behave as they do.
    Texture control is a quiet pillar of manufacturing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The limits of Hall–Petch.</strong> Shrink grains to a few nanometres and the
    relation reverses — <em>inverse</em> Hall–Petch — because below about 10–15 nm, grain
    boundary sliding takes over as the deformation mechanism and boundaries (previously
    obstacles) become the weakness. The strongest microstructure is neither coarse nor
    ultrafine; there is an optimum, a nice example of a design rule that has a domain of
    validity stamped into it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Dislocations were once a theory.</strong> In 1934, Orowan, Polanyi and Taylor
    each proposed dislocations to explain the strength gap; the metal community resisted for
    two decades — until dislocations were photographed directly in the early 1950s by
    transmission electron microscopy, exactly as predicted. A cautionary tale about how long
    a correct mechanism can wait for its observation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Predicting a new alloy's structure and properties
    purely by computation (the CALPHAD and DFT-to-machine-learning pipeline) is the field's
    grand project — a couple of hundred thousand known compounds against a combinatorial
    space of 10<sup>60</sup> or more candidates. High-entropy alloys (five metals in equal
    parts, nobody's recipe) broke the folk rule that alloys need one dominant element.
    And amorphous-to-crystal transitions still hide surprises, from quasicrystals
    (Shechtman's 1982 discovery of forbidden five-fold symmetry, Nobel 2011, initially
    thrown out of his own lab as impossible) to metallic glasses that have not yet found
    their mass-market application.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Callister &amp; Rethwisch, Materials Science and Engineering; Ashby, Materials Selection in Mechanical Design; Cahn &amp; Haasen, Physical Metallurgy; the chemistry ladder's bonding lesson and the physics ladder's solid-state lesson.</p>

